THOUGHT: Now I understand the vulnerability. Look at `HF_strlcat`:

```c
static inline size_t HF_strlcat(char* dest, const char* src, size_t sz, uintptr_t addr) {
    size_t len = __builtin_strlen(dest);
    return HF_strlcpy(dest + len, src, sz, addr);
}
```

And `HF_strlcpy`:

```c
static inline size_t HF_strlcpy(char* dest, const char* src, size_t sz, uintptr_t addr) {
    size_t slen = __builtin_strlen(src);
    size_t len  = sz < slen ? sz : slen;

    if (sz == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    /* Make space for NUL at the end of the string.
     * sz != 0 here
     */
    if (len == sz) {
        len--;
    }

    if (len > 0) {
        instrumentUpdateCmpMap(addr, util_Log2(len));
        (void)__builtin_memcpy(dest, src, len);
    }

    dest[len] = '\0';
    return len;
}
```

The bug in `HF_strlcat` is that it passes `sz` (the full buffer size) to `HF_strlcpy` as the size, but `dest + len` is already `len` bytes into the buffer. So `HF_strlcpy` will write up to `sz` bytes starting at `dest + len`, which overflows the buffer by `len` bytes.

In the `read_config` code:
```c
char fname[SNMP_MAXPATH];  // 1024 bytes on stack
strlcpy(fname, filename, SNMP_MAXPATH);  // filename is short, e.g. "/tmp/libfuzzer-123.config"
cp = strrchr(fname, '/');
*(++cp) = '\0';  // fname = "/tmp/"
strlcat(fname, cptr, SNMP_MAXPATH);  // cptr is attacker-controlled
```

So `fname` = "/tmp/" (5 chars), and `strlcat(fname, cptr, 1024)` will call `HF_strlcat(fname, cptr, 1024)` which does `HF_strlcpy(fname + 5, cptr, 1024)`. This will copy up to 1023 bytes starting at `fname + 5`, overflowing the 1024-byte stack buffer by ~5 bytes.

To trigger this, I need to provide a config file with an `includefile` token followed by a very long path (close to or exceeding `SNMP_MAXPATH` = 1024 bytes). The `cptr` needs to be long enough that `strlcat` writes past the end of `fname`.

Let me create the PoC. The config file should contain:
```
includefile <very long path>
```

The path needs to be long enough to overflow. Since `fname` starts with "/tmp/" (5 chars), and `strlcat` will copy up to `SNMP_MAXPATH - 1` = 1023 bytes from `cptr` starting at `fname + 5`, we need `cptr` to be at least 1024 - 5 = 1019 bytes to overflow the buffer.

